%% ROW_ROLE_DEFNS_DRAFT.ott
%%
%% Minimal row + role judgement draft, in ett.ott-style syntax.
%%
%% This is NOT an attempt to extend corespec-roles/spec/ett.ott itself.
%% It is a polytropos-local formal sketch, written in the same surface
%% syntax so that the judgement shapes are concrete and reviewable.
%%
%% Intent / scope (matches ROW_ROLE_JUDGEMENT_SKETCH.md §5):
%%   - closed vs open row kinds
%%   - HKD-style functor parameter per row field (future-oriented): a row
%%     whose entry kind is @f ty@ for some field functor @f@, so the same
%%     judgement vocabulary can express product/variant rows without fixing
%%     @f ~ Identity@.
%%   - row inclusion  r1 ⊆ r2
%%   - a couple of widening rules
%%   - the two-point role lattice Nom ≤ Rep
%%   - role-subsumption for row constraints (the Sub-style rule)
%%
%% Deliberately left out (see ROW_ROLE_JUDGEMENT_SKETCH.md §4):
%%   - per-label roles
%%   - full row-polymorphic quantification
%%   - row pattern matching
%%   - full ICFP 2019 dependent-type apparatus
%%   - OCaml's Reither / object-row encoding
%%   - any completeness/soundness claim
%%
%% NOTE on HKD-style functor parameters (open extension):
%%   The judgement layer below is deliberately phrased at the row-syntax
%%   level (label :> type), not at the GHC vocabulary level (label := f ty).
%%   That is intentional: the judgement does not need to know the GHC
%%   spelling of the entry kind in order to state inclusion/equality/widening.
%%   When polytropos adopts HKD-aware rows (for example ProductRowHKD f,
%%   SumRowHKD f, or a RowType-flavored variant), the entry-kind change is
%%   a vocabulary change in the type layer, not a judgement change here.
%%   Concretely:
%%     - current shipped vocabulary:  Row    = [Pair Symbol Type]
%%                                  SumRow = [Pair Symbol Row]
%%     - HKD-aware target vocabulary (sketch, not committed spelling):
%%         ProductRowHKD f = [Pair Symbol (f Type)]
%%         SumRowHKD f     = [Pair Symbol (ProductRowHKD f)]
%%   The draft leaves the exact spelling open (see PLAN.md / spec sketch
%%   §7.1) and only commits to the judgement-level property that the entry
%%   kind may be parameterized by a field functor, so the same inclusion/
%%   equality/widening judgements can be reused across @f@ instantiations.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 0. Reference notes (not part of Ott grammar)
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% Row algebra source (row side — OCaml, not GHC):
%%   OCaml typechecker, typing/ctype.ml:
%%     merge_row_fields / merge_rf
%%     subtype_row
%%     unify_row / unify_row_field
%%     row_rep, row_more, row_closed, row_fixed
%%     static_row
%%   Plus the polytropos note:
%%     docs/notes/OCAML_ROW_TAIL_SUBTYPE_MECHANISM.md
%%   What this is: the row-side anchor. Not GHC. The draft's row judgements
%%   (inclusion, widening, the tail contract) are aligned with this, not
%%   derived from GHC's constraint solver. Secondary theoretical support for
%%   the width-subtype-vs-equality distinction: Dolan, Algebraic Subtyping
%%   (PhD thesis); Parreaux, The Simple Essence of Algebraic Subtyping.
%%
%% Role/erasure source (role side — ICFP 2019 corespec-roles, not GHC):
%%   Weirich et al., "A Role for Dependent Types in Haskell" (ICFP 2019)
%%   and its Coq/Ott development at ~/src/corespec-roles.
%%   Imported pieces:
%%     - two-role lattice Nom/Rep with <= R1 R2  (corespec JSubRole)
%%     - definitional equality a == b : A / R
%%     - Sub rule for role-subsumption
%%     - separate roleing judgement
%%     - erase_tm / erase_constraint
%%   What this is: the role-side anchor for the LATTICE and the SUBSUMPTION
%%   RULE only. It does NOT claim GHC's implementation is identical to this;
%%   it claims the apparatus (Nom/Rep, role-indexed equality, Sub) is the
%%   same class of apparatus GHC already has. The GHC-native claim for the
%%   constraint FORM (~ at a role) is in §10's citation note, not here.
%%
%% GHC-native anchor (constraint form — GHC, not corespec):
%%   The row-equality constraint form r1 ~ r2 : A / R is GHC's existing
%%   equality predicate parameterized by a role. The draft does NOT invent
%%   a new constraint kind. The canonical citation is GHC's own source:
%%     - compiler/Language/Haskell/Syntax/Basic.hs: data Role = Nominal |
%%       Representational | Phantom (constructor order = superotyping rel)
%%     - compiler/GHC/Core/Predicate.hs: data EqRel = NomEq | ReprEq;
%%       eqRelRole :: EqRel -> Role; mkEqPredRole :: Role -> Type -> Type
%%       -> PredType; mkNomEqPred; mkReprEqPred; the EqPred EqRel Type Type
%%       constructor of data Pred
%%     - compiler/GHC/Core/TyCo/Rep.hs: Note [Roles] (points to
%%       docs/core-spec/core-spec.pdf and
%%       https://gitlab.haskell.org/ghc/ghc/wikis/roles-implementation,
%%       and to Weirich et al., Generative Type Abstraction and Type-level
%%       Computation, POPL 2010)
%%     - compiler/GHC/Core/Coercion.hs: Note [Roles] on the coercion side;
%%       SubCo (Nom→Rep demotion); role-parameterized coercion constructors
%%   The GHC-side analogue of the draft's / R role index is EqRel (NomEq /
%%   ReprEq) + mkEqPredRole. The Nom ≤ Rep direction is realized in GHC by
%%   SubCo (nominal-to-representational demotion) plus the role-parameterized
%%   coercion constructors — the same class of apparatus the corespec Sub
%%   rule abstracts.
%%   The full GHC roles specification is in docs/core-spec/core-spec.pdf
%%   within the GHC tree. The draft's lattice (§9) is a TWO-role lattice;
%%   GHC's is a THREE-role lattice (Nominal/Representational/Phantom).
%%   The draft does NOT claim the two are identical; it claims the Nom ≤ Rep
%%   direction GHC already has is the direction our Sub rule uses.
%%   The GHC citation is the canonical source for "the plugin emits ~ constraints
%%   at roles, which GHC already supports." See docs/notes/REVIEWER_WFU_
%%   ARGUMENT.md §2.3.
%%     - separate roleing judgement
%%     - erase_tm / erase_constraint

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 1. Metavariables
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% We do not re-define the full ICFP 2019 metavar set here.
%% We only introduce the metavariables this draft needs.

metavar label, l, m, n ::= {{ com row labels }}
  {{ lex alphanum }}

metavar tvar, ρ, α, β ::= {{ com row tail variables }}
  {{ lex alphanum }}

indexvar index, i ::= {{ com literal positions }}
  {{ tex \mathit{[[index]]} }}
  {{ coq nat }}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 2. Row kinds
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% A row is either closed or open.
%%
%% This is the OCaml-flavored split:
%%   closed row  ~ row_fields only, tail effectively Tnil / fixed
%%   open row    ~ row_fields plus a tail variable ρ
%%
%% In this draft we treat a row as a separate syntactic category because
%% polytropos's current shipped row vocabulary has no row tail at all.
%% The immediate goal of this draft is to state that missing piece
%% concretely, not to claim it is already implemented.

row, r, s ::= {{ com rows }}

  %% closed row: finite ordered map from labels to types.
  %% Corresponds to OCaml's static-row/end-of-row case where tail
  %% is not a unification variable.
  ( l :> A  ,  r )        ::   :: RowCons

  %% empty closed row. Corresponds to an empty known field set.
  ()                      ::   :: RowNil

  %% open row: known fields plus a row tail variable.
  %% The tail stands for "zero or more additional unknown labels".
  ( l :> A  ,  r )  ρ     :: S :: RowOpen

  %% An open row written with an explicit tail binder, for use in
  %% contexts where we want the tail variable to be syntactically
  %% apparent. This is the shape we expect the plugin/constraint
  %% layer to manipulate.
  ρ                       ::   :: RowTail

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 3. Row inclusion judgement — Reading B (constraint-emission-aware)
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% GOAL (from the session decision):
%%   The inclusion judgement fires on immediate shapes AND on open rows
%%   whose tails are NOT yet solved. When it encounters an unsolved tail,
%%   it does NOT solve the tail itself; it delegates the unsolved tail to
%%   constraint emission.

%% Central judgement of this draft:
%%
%%     r1 ⊆ r2
%%
%% Read: a value whose row is r1 can be used where a value whose row
%% is r2 is expected.
%%
%% This is NOT equality. It is the relation computed (with constraints)
%% by OCaml's subtype_row after merging fields with merge_row_fields.
%%
%% Why Reading B (vs Reading A):
%%   Reading A (judgement over immediate shapes only) would say:
%%     "r1 ⊆ r2 only applies when both r1 and r2 are concrete closed rows,
%%      or when the tail relationship is already known."
%%   Reading B says:
%%     "r1 ⊆ r2 applies whenever the known-field decomposition succeeds,
%%      EVEN IF the tails are still variables. The tail relationship becomes
%%      a constraint that the constraint layer discharges later."
%%   Reading B is required for row-polymorphic functions to use the
%%   judgement, because a row-polymorphic function's argument row is a
%%   variable, not a concrete shape.
%%
%% Important design alignment with OCaml:
%%   - Inclusion decomposes label-by-label after the two field sets are
%%     aligned.
%%   - Leftover fields on one side are handled by the open/closed shape
%%     of the other side's tail.
%%   - The tail is not magic; inclusion does not "unify away" a closed row
%%     into an open one.
%%   Alignment: OCaml merge_row_fields / subtype_row (row side, not GHC).
%%   See docs/notes/OCAML_ROW_TAIL_SUBTYPE_MECHANISM.md and §0.

defn
r1 ⊆ r2 ::   :: RowIncl :: 'RowIncl_'
{{ com row inclusion }}
{{ tex [[r1]] \\subseteq [[r2]] }}
by

%% Empty closed rows are included in anything that admits no
%% extra-label mismatch on the closed side.
%% This is the empty-closed-row base case (closed-to-closed nil).
()
  ⊆ r

%% A known field must appear on the right with a compatible type.
%% In this draft, "compatible type" is represented by equality A == B
%% at the relevant role. This is the label-by-label decomposition
%% that OCaml's merge_row_fields + subtype_row performs.
%%   Alignment: OCaml merge_row_fields / subtype_row (row side, not GHC).
%%   See docs/notes/OCAML_ROW_TAIL_SUBTYPE_MECHANISM.md and §0.
%%
%% NOTE: This rule applies once per matching label, regardless of whether
%% the remaining tails are closed or open. The judgement does not require
%% the tails to be solved before recursing.
%%   Alignment: OCaml merge_row_fields / subtype_row (row side, not GHC).
%%   See docs/notes/OCAML_ROW_TAIL_SUBTYPE_MECHANISM.md and §0.
( l :> A  ,  r1 )  ⊆  ( l :> B  ,  r2 )
  -----------------------------------------
  r1 ⊆ r2

%% Closed-to-open widening.
%% A fully known closed row can be widened to an open row whose known
%% part is at least as big. In OCaml terms, this is the direction
%% where a static row can be seen as an open row with a fresh/bound
%% tail variable.
%%
%% Under Reading B, this rule is a widening rule inside the judgement,
%% NOT a claim that the closed row was already open. See §8.
r  ⊆  ( r )  ρ

%% Open-to-open inclusion — Reading B version.
%% Two open rows are included when their known fields align and their
%% tails can be merged.
%%
%% Under Reading B, the tail merge is NOT performed by this judgement.
%% The judgement emits the tail relationship ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 as a constraint for
%% the constraint layer to discharge later. This is the "both sides have
%% row variable" case that Reading A would have refused or made conditional.
%%
%% Falsifiability test for Reading B:
%%   If a row-polymorphic function f : ∀ ρ. { a : Int | ρ } → ... is applied
%%   to an argument whose row is { a : Int, b : String | ρ' }, the judgement
%%   must be able to fire without ρ and ρ' already being unified. Reading B
%%   allows this because the tail relationship becomes a constraint, not a
%%   precondition on the judgement.
%%   Alignment: OCaml subtype_row deferred-constraint behavior for non-
%%   comparable tails (row side, not GHC). See OCAML_ROW_TAIL_SUBTYPE_
%%   MECHANISM.md and §0.
( r1 )  ρ1  ⊆  ( r2 )  ρ2
  -----------------------------
  r1 ⊆ r2      ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2

%% Closed-row inclusion into an open row with a DIFFERENT known part.
%% This is the case Reading A often mishandles: a closed row { a : Int }
%% being used where an open row { b : String | ρ } is expected.
%%
%% Under Reading B, this becomes two constraints:
%%   1. The known-field decomposition may fail (if a label is missing on
%%      the open side), in which case inclusion fails.
%%   2. If the known fields are compatible, the closed tail must widen to
%%      the open tail, which is a tail judgement ρ_closed ⊆₊ ρ_open.
%% This rule is stated here to expose the case explicitly; whether it is
%% derivable from the rules above is a separate question (see §12).
%% NOTE: This rule is deliberately provisional. See §12.
( l1 :> A  ,  r1 )  ⊆  ( l2 :> B  ,  r2 )  ρ
  ---------------------------------------------
  ... (label compatibility condition) ...   r1 ⊆ ( r2 ) ρ   ρ_closed ⊆₊ ρ

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 4. Row-tail interaction judgements — §2/§3 boundary contract
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% GOAL (from the session decisions):
%%   - The row-shape layer is row-polymorphic in its tails, so the tail is
%%     NOT resolved inside the row-shape layer.
%%   - The inclusion judgement (§3) therefore fires on open rows whose tails
%%     are NOT yet solved, and leaves the tail unsolved.
%%   - The row-shape layer hands the tail to the constraint layer as a thing
%%     the constraint layer has to carry.
%%   - The constraint layer emits deferred constraints / unifier work to solve
%%     the tail, rather than the inclusion judgement solving it inline.
%%
%% This is the §2/§3 boundary contract stated together, because the two
%% decisions are the same decision seen from opposite sides of the boundary.

%% Terminology (used throughout §4 and §5):
%%   tail-nil   — static closed tail, no further labels possible
%%   tail-var ρ — open tail that can be widened/merged under constraint
%%   tail-fixed — closed/fixed tail that must not be widened

defn
ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 ::   :: RowTailIncl :: 'RowTailIncl_'
{{ com row tail inclusion judgement }}

%% THIS JUDGEMENT IS DECLARED, NOT SOLVED HERE.
%% The row-shape layer uses ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 to state that two open tails are
%% compatible under widening. The constraint layer is responsible for
%% actually discharging that obligation. See §4 for how the hand-off works.

%% Two identical open tail variables are compatible.
%% Constraint layer: may treat the tail as compatible without inventing a new
%% row. No constraint emission needed if the two sides are already the same
%% variable — the tail is already shared.
ρ  ⊆₊  ρ

%% An open tail variable may absorb a known closed remainder.
%% Constraint layer: this is the construction that makes open rows genuinely
%% open. Allowed, but as widening under constraint, not as unconditional row
%% equality. Emitting this as a constraint means the tail variable stays
%% unsolved until the closed remainder is accounted for.
ρ  ⊆₊  ()

%% A fresh open tail may be compatible with an existing tail variable.
%% Constraint layer: this is the widening step on the tail itself. The
%% judgement allows it; the plugin gates actual acceptance behind constraint
%% emission. Two distinct tail variables are NOT made equal by this judgement
%% alone — they become equal only if the constraint layer unifies them.
ρ  ⊆₊  ρ'

%% Closed/fixed tails must NOT be widened.
%% Constraint layer: if either side is a closed/fixed tail, the plugin:
%%   - must NOT turn it into an open tail
%%   - must NOT accept leftover fields against it unless it is already an
%%     open shape compatible under constraint
%% This mirrors OCaml's static_row / fixed_explanation gating.
ρ  ⊆₊  ρ'       when ρ is not tail-fixed, ρ' is tail-var

%% Non-identical closed/fixed tails are NOT silently merged.
%% Constraint layer: if both sides are closed/fixed and not identical, the
%% plugin either emits a deferred constraint or rejects the inclusion, exactly
%% as OCaml's subtype_row does for non-comparable tail constructors.
(ρ1 is tail-fixed, ρ2 is tail-fixed, ρ1 ≠ ρ2)
  ⊆₊  ρ2      ← deferred constraint or reject

%% HOW THE BOUNDARY HAND-OFF WORKS (§2/§3)
%%
%% When the inclusion judgement (§3) sees two open rows whose tails differ,
%% it does not try to solve the tail relationship. Instead:
%%   1. The row-shape layer decomposes the known fields (label-by-label).
%%   2. For the leftover tails, the row-shape layer emits a tail judgement
%%      ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 into the constraint layer's context.
%%   3. The constraint layer treats ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 as a constraint it must later
%%      discharge — by unifying the two tail variables, by widening one tail
%%      under the tail contract above, or by rejecting the inclusion if the
%%      tail shapes are non-comparable.
%%
%% This is what "handing the tail off to the constraint layer as a thing the
%% constraint layer has to carry" means in practice: the tail does not escape
%% the system, but it does escape the row-shape layer. The constraint layer
%% owns the tail until it is solved or rejected.
%%   Alignment (row side, not GHC): OCaml subtype_row deferred-constraint
%%   behavior. See OCAML_ROW_TAIL_SUBTYPE_MECHANISM.md and §0.

%% Operational note on constraint emission
%%
%% "Constraint emission" is the act of the plugin adding a wanted constraint
%% to GHC's constraint solver. In this draft it means: when the inclusion
%% judgement leaves a tail judgement unresolved, the plugin drops
%% ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 into the solver as a constraint the solver must discharge. The
%% plugin does NOT attempt to discharge it itself; that is the solver's job,
%% gated by the tail contract above.
%%
%% This is the plugin-boundary analogue of OCaml's subtype_row aborting to a
%% deferred constraint when the tails are not directly comparable.
%%   Alignment (row side, not GHC): OCaml subtype_row deferred-constraint
%%   behavior. See OCAML_ROW_TAIL_SUBTYPE_MECHANISM.md and §0.
%%   The constraint FORM itself (a ~ b at a role) is GHC-native, not OCaml.
%%   See §10's citation note and docs/notes/REVIEWER_WFU_ARGUMENT.md.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 5. Layer-appropriate specialization and the shared row-algebra layer
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% GOAL (from the session decision):
%%   The row algebra is factored into a third shared layer so that the
%%   row-shape layer and the constraint layer can each use layer-appropriate
%%   specializations of the same algebra without duplicating it.

%% Why this is a design decision, not an implementation detail:
%%   The row algebra (row union, row difference, label-set operations,
%%   tail construction) is used by at least two layers with different
%%   correctness criteria:
%%     - The row-shape / representation layer uses the algebra to construct
%%       Rep arrays and to decide field indices. It cares about layout
%%       performance, array compactness, and correct index assignment.
%%     - The constraint layer uses the algebra to decide what obligations to
%%       emit. It cares about decidability, termination, and soundness of
%%       the inclusion judgement — NOT about how fast an array gets laid out.
%%   If the two layers share a single implementation of the algebra, then
%%   either the representation layer's performance choices leak into the
%%   constraint layer, or the constraint layer's generality requirements
%%   force representation-layer overhead that is not needed.
%%   If the two layers each own a copy of the algebra, they can drift apart
%%   and the inclusion judgement can silently disagree with the actual
%%   representation layout.

%% The third shared layer:
%%   The candidate resolution is to factor the row-algebra primitives into
%%   a layer that both the row-shape layer and the constraint layer depend
%%   on, but that neither owns exclusively.
%%   In that arrangement:
%%     - The shared layer exposes the algebra as PURE operations on row
%%       shapes: union, difference, label membership, tail construction,
%%       closed vs open discrimination.
%%     - The row-shape layer uses those primitives to decide indices and
%%       to construct Rep.
%%     - The constraint layer uses the SAME primitives to decompose
%%       inclusion and to emit tail judgements, but applies its own
%%       constraint-emission policy on top.
%%   This is the cleanest dependency topology, but it is also the one that
%%   requires the most infrastructure before anything is working: you need
%%   the shared layer to exist before either consumer can be finished.

%% Trade-off statement (for the record):
%%   The alternative to the third shared layer is one of:
%%     (a) Row-shape layer owns the algebra; constraint layer calls in.
%%         Risk: constraint layer becomes dependent on representation-layer
%%         internals that it does not need.
%%     (b) Constraint layer owns the algebra; row-shape layer calls in.
%%         Risk: representation layer pays for generality it does not use.
%%     (c) Both own a copy. Risk: drift, silent disagreement.
%%   The session decision is to prefer (d) a third shared layer, with the
%%   understanding that (d) may be over-engineering if the algebra stays
%%   small enough. The draft leaves that judgment open.

%% This section is NOT a rule. It is the §5 design decision stated so that
%% later commits can check whether the code matches it.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 6. Row equality judgement — distinct from inclusion, with tail exactness
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% GOAL (from the session decision):
%%   Add a row equality judgement that is DISTINCT from bidirectional
%%   inclusion, with tail exactness, so that nominal equivalence at the row
%%   level can be stated precisely even where it complicates the inference
%%   rule set.
%%
%% Motivation (Haskell nominal-type backward compatibility):
%%   Haskell's declared nominal types rely on the ability to state that two
%%   row shapes are equal in the strict sense required for nominal equality:
%%   same known fields, same types at the relevant role, AND tails that are
%%   exactly the same — not merely mutually compatible under widening.
%%   Without a distinct equality judgement, the draft can only state
%%   inclusion (directional, widening-allowed) and a row-equality constraint
%%   form with role-subsumption (§11), but cannot certify that two open rows
%%   with different tail variables are or are not nominally equal.
%%   For the shipped literal-index plugin, this matters because a literal
%%   index assignment is only sound if the row shape is exactly what the
%%   plugin thinks it is — which is an exactness property, not a
%%   compatibility property.
%%   Alignment: the width-subtype-vs-equality distinction is the row-side
%%   theoretical support (Dolan, Algebraic Subtyping; Parreaux, Simple
%%   Essence of Algebraic Subtyping). See §0. The exactness-vs-compatibility
%%   split is embodied here in ≃₊ vs ⊆₊ (§7 vs §4).

defn
r1 ≃ r2  ::  :: RowEqJ :: 'RowEqJ_'
{{ com row equality judgement }}
{{ tex [[r1]] \\simeq [[r2]] }}

%% Empty closed rows are equal.
()  ≃  ()

%% Known fields must match exactly, label-by-label, with type equality at
%% the relevant role, and the tails must be exactly equal (not merely
%% compatible).
%%
%% This is the label-by-label decomposition shared with inclusion (§3), but
%% with equality on the tail instead of inclusion.
%%   Alignment (row side, not GHC): OCaml merge_row_fields / subtype_row.
%%   See docs/notes/OCAML_ROW_TAIL_SUBTYPE_MECHANISM.md and §0.
%%   - The type-equality A == B is at the role relevant to the context in
%%     which the equality judgement is invoked. See §9 (role lattice) and
%%     §11 (role-subsumption on the row-equality constraint form).
%%     Alignment (role side, not GHC): ICFP 2019 corespec-roles JSubRole /
%%     role-indexed equality a == b : A / R. See §0 and
%%     docs/notes/REVIEWER_WFU_ARGUMENT.md.
%%   - The tail equality ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2 is STRICT: two distinct tail variables are
%%     NOT equal, even if both are open. Tail equality requires either the
%%     same variable or unification by the constraint layer.
%%     Alignment: this is the exactness half of the two-tail-judgement design.
%%     See §6 above and §0.
%%     Alignment: this is the exactness judgement that complements the
%%     compatibility judgement ⊆₊ (§4). The two-tail-judgement design is the
%%     row-specific embodiment of the width-subtype-vs-equality distinction.
%%     See §0 and §6 above.
( l :> A  ,  r1 )  ≃  ( l :> B  ,  r2 )
  -----------------------------------------
  A == B        r1 ≃ r2        ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2

%% NOTE: This rule is stated for the case where the row syntax carries a
%% tail variable (open row). For closed rows (no tail variable in the
%% syntax), the tail-equality premise is vacuous — see the closed-row
%% equality rules below.

%% Closedrow equality — exact, no tail variable.
%% Two closed rows are equal when their known fields match exactly and
%% there is no tail variable to compare (the tail is empty/fixed).
%%
%% This is the exactness property the session decision wanted: a closed row
%% is equal to another closed row only when they are structurally identical,
%% including the absent tail. This is what nominal equality requires.
( l1 :> A  ,  r1 )  ≃  ( l2 :> B  ,  r2 )
  -----------------------------------------
  l1 = l2        A == B        r1 ≃ r2

%% Two empty closed rows are equal (already stated above as () ≃ (), but
%% repeated here for the closed-row decomposition completeness).
()  ≃  ()

%% Closedrow equality does NOT hold between a closed row and an open row
%% with the same known fields. The open row's tail is not known to be empty,
%% so the exactness condition fails.
%%
%% This is the case that would be wrong under a bidirectional-inclusion
%% definition of equality: { a : Int } is included in { a : Int | ρ }, and
%% { a : Int | ρ } includes { a : Int } only if ρ ⊆₊ (), which ⊆₊ allows
%% (open tail absorbs closed nil, §4). So bidirectional inclusion would
%% declare them equal. The equality judgement does not, because the open
%% row's tail ρ is not ≃₊ ().
%%
%% Plugin-facing test: a literal-index plugin must NOT treat { a : Int } and
%% { a : Int | ρ } as the same row shape, even if ρ is later unified with
%% the empty tail. The equality judgement refuses until ρ ≃₊ (), which
%% requires ρ to be unified with the empty tail — at which point ρ is no
%% longer a distinct variable and the two rows are both closed.
( l :> A  ,  r1 )  ≃  ( l :> B  ,  r2 )  ρ
  -----------------------------------------
  (not derivable — closed row ≠ open row at nominal equality)

%% Openrow equality — tail exactness required.
%% Two open rows are equal when their known fields match exactly AND their
%% tails are exactly equal (ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2).
%%
%% This is what distinguishes equality from bidirectional inclusion:
%%   - (r1) ρ1 ⊆ (r2) ρ2  AND  (r2) ρ2 ⊆ (r1) ρ1  can both hold with
%%     ρ1 ≠ ρ2 (mutual widening compatibility, §4), so bidirectional
%%     inclusion would wrongly declare them equal.
%%   - (r1) ρ1 ≃ (r2) ρ2  requires ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2, which requires ρ1 = ρ2 or
%%     solver unification, so the equality judgement is sound for nominal
%%     use.
( r1 )  ρ1  ≃  ( r2 )  ρ2
  -----------------------------------------
  r1 ≃ r2        ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2

%% Summary of the exactness distinction:
%%   Inclusion  ⊆   : tail relationship is  ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2  (compatibility under
%%                     widening; distinct open tails CAN be mutually
%%                     compatible).
%%   Equality   ≃   : tail relationship is  ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2  (exactness; distinct
%%                     open tails are NOT equal).
%%   Bidirectional inclusion  (⊆ ∧ ⊆)  would use ⊆₊ in both directions,
%%   which does not imply ≃₊. Hence equality cannot be defined as mutual
%%   inclusion without adding exactness to the inclusion judgement, which
%%   would break the Reading B design (§3).

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 7. Tail equality judgement — strict, distinct from tail inclusion
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% GOAL (from the session decision):
%%   Add a tail equality judgement ≃₊ that is STRICT, distinct from the
%%   tail inclusion judgement ⊆₊ (§4).
%%
%% This is the explicit cost the session decision accepted: having two tail
%% judgements, one for compatibility (⊆₊, used by inclusion) and one for
%% exactness (≃₊, used by equality), which must be kept consistent by the
%% condition in the consistency rule below.

defn
ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2  ::  :: TailEqJ :: 'TailEqJ_'
{{ com tail equality judgement — strict }}

%% Two identical open tail variables are equal.
ρ  ≃₊  ρ

%% Two distinct open tail variables are NOT equal.
%% This is the exactness requirement. Unlike ⊆₊ (§4), which allows
%% ρ ⊆₊ ρ' for distinct ρ, ρ', the equality judgement does not.
%%
%% Constraint layer: if the plugin needs to establish ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2 for distinct
%% tail variables, it must ask the solver to unify them. If unification
%% succeeds and yields ρ1 = ρ2, then ≃₊ holds. If unification fails or
%% yields distinct variables, ≃₊ does not hold.
%%     Alignment: this is the exactness half of the two-tail-judgement design.
%%     See §6 above and §0.
ρ  ≃₊  ρ'       (when ρ ≠ ρ')  ← NOT derivable; requires solver unification

%% A tail variable is NOT equal to the closed nil tail.
%% This is the exactness requirement: an open tail is not known to be empty.
%%
%% Contrast with ⊆₊ (§4): ρ ⊆₊ () IS allowed (open tail absorbs closed nil,
%% as a widening step). ≃₊ does NOT allow it, because the open tail is not
%% known to be empty.
ρ  ≃₊  ()       ← NOT derivable

%% Closed/fixed tails: equality requires identity.
%% If both sides are closed/fixed, they are equal only if they are identical.
%% This mirrors the closed-row equality exactness condition above.
ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2       when ρ1 is tail-fixed, ρ2 is tail-fixed, ρ1 = ρ2

%% Non-identical closed/fixed tails are NOT equal.
(ρ1 is tail-fixed, ρ2 is tail-fixed, ρ1 ≠ ρ2)
  ≃₊  ρ2      ← NOT derivable

%% Relationship between ⊆₊ and ≃₊ (consistency constraint):
%%   If ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2 then ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 and ρ2 ⊆₊ ρ1.
%%   The converse does NOT hold: mutual ⊆₊ does not imply ≃₊.
%%   This is the consistency condition that keeps the two tail judgements
%%   from drifting. The draft commits to stating it, not to having enforced
%%   it mechanically.
%%
%% Plugin boundary note:
%%   The plugin should maintain this consistency invariant: whenever it
%%   establishes ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2 (for the equality judgement), it must also have
%%   established ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 and ρ2 ⊆₊ ρ1 (for the inclusion judgement),
%%   because ≃₊ is strictly stronger. The converse is not required and
%%   would be wrong.
ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2
-------------
ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2        ρ2 ⊆₊ ρ1

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 8. Widening as a derived/direct rule
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% Widening is the constructive direction of inclusion.
%%
%% For this draft we state it directly rather than as a separate
%% metatheorem, because the point is to have something falsifiable:
%% the judgement set should be able to express "closed row → open row".
%%
%% Plugin boundary note:
%%   Widening in the judgement language is not the same as the plugin
%%   deciding a row is already widened. The plugin should treat widening
%%   as a controlled transition:
%%     - closed row → open row may be allowed when the row is not fixed
%%     - tail widening is allowed only through the tail contract in §4
%%     - once a row is fixed/closed, narrowing may be attempted but
%%       widening back out is not allowed by fiat

defn
r ⇝ w                ::   :: RowWiden :: 'RowWiden_'
{{ com row widening }}
{{ tex [[r]] \\rightsquigarrow [[w]] }}

%% Widen a closed row to an open row by attaching a tail variable.
r  ⇝  ( r )  ρ

%% Widen a tail variable into a larger tail variable.
%% This is the tail-only widening analogue.
ρ  ⇝  ρ'

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 9. Role lattice
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% We adopt the two-point role lattice from ICFP 2019.
%%
%%   Nom  -- nominal equality only; no coercion through abstraction
%%   Rep  -- representational equality; coercion-safe
%%
%% This is the lattice already present in corespec-roles/spec/ett.ott
%% as JSubRole / SubRole. We restate it here only so this draft is
%% self-contained.
%%   Alignment (role side, not GHC): ICFP 2019 corespec-roles JSubRole.
%%   See §0 and docs/notes/REVIEWER_WFU_ARGUMENT.md.

defn
R1 ≤ R2  ::  :: SubRole :: 'SubRole_'
{{ com role subsumption }}
{{ tex [[R1]] \\leq [[R2]] }}

------------ :: NomBot
Nom  ≤  R

------------ :: RepTop
R  ≤  Rep

------------ :: Refl
R  ≤  R

R1 ≤ R2
R2 ≤ R3
--------- :: Trans
R1 ≤ R3

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 10. Row constraints
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% Minimal row constraint forms for this draft.
%%
%% We do not commit to a full constraint language yet. We only add the
%% two shapes needed for the immediate question:
%%   - row equality
%%   - row inclusion
%%
%% The role index is kept on equality only, because the minimal
%% hypothesis is that inclusion is a structural relation, while
%% equality/coercion evidence may need a role.

constraint, φ ::= {{ com row constraints }}

  r1 ~ r2 : A / R     ::   :: RowEq
  {{ com row equality at role R }}
%%   Alignment (constraint form — GHC, not corespec): this is GHC's existing
%%   equality constraint (~) parameterized by a role. The draft does NOT
%%   invent a new constraint kind. The canonical citation for "GHC already
%%   supports ~ at a role" is GHC's own source for equality constraints and
%%   roles, NOT corespec (which is the anchor for the lattice/subsumption
%%   apparatus only). See §0 and docs/notes/REVIEWER_WFU_ARGUMENT.md.

  r1 ⊆ r2             ::   :: RowInclC
  {{ com row inclusion constraint }}

  ( φ )               :: S :: RowCParen

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 11. Role-subsumption for row constraints
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% The key rule that imports the role lattice into the row-constraint
%% layer. It mirrors ICFP 2019's Sub rule, but restricted to the row
%% constraint forms this draft has.
%%   Alignment (role side, not GHC): ICFP 2019 corespec-roles Sub rule.
%%   See §0 and docs/notes/REVIEWER_WFU_ARGUMENT.md.
%%
%% This is where closed-row equality vs closed-open compatibility may
%% acquire different roles:
%%   - exact closed-row equality is likely Nom
%%   - closed-row/compatible-open compatibility may be Rep under inclusion

defn
φ1 ≤ φ2  ::  :: RowConstrSub :: 'RowConstrSub_'
{{ com row constraint subsumption }}

%% Role-subsumption on row equality, exactly the ICFP 2019 Sub shape.
%%   Alignment (role side, not GHC): ICFP 2019 corespec-roles Sub rule.
%%   See §0 and docs/notes/REVIEWER_WFU_ARGUMENT.md.
r ~ s : A / R1      R1 ≤ R2
---------------------------------
r ~ s : A / R2

%% Inclusion constraints do not change by role alone in this minimal
%% draft. That is a design choice, not a theorem. The draft leaves a
%% gap here on purpose: whether inclusion itself should be role-indexed
%% is one of the things this draft is meant to expose.
r1 ⊆ r2              r1 ⊆ r2
---------------------------------
r1 ⊆ r2

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 12. What this draft does and does not yet commit to
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% Commits to:
%%   - a closed vs open row distinction in the syntax
%%   - row inclusion as a judgement, not equality (§3)
%%   - a row equality judgement distinct from inclusion, with tail exactness
%%     (§6), and a strict tail equality judgement ≃₊ (§7)
%%   - at least one closed-to-open widening rule (§8)
%%   - the Nom ≤ Rep lattice (§9)
%%   - a role-subsumption rule for row equality (§11)
 (§11)
%%
%% Does not commit to:
%%   - whether polytropos will actually adopt row tails in code
%%   - how the tail judgement ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 is solved
%%   - per-label roles
%%   - row polymorphism
%%   - any claim that the above is a complete or proved system
%%
%% Gap CLOSED by §6/§7 (session decision: distinct equality judgement with
%% tail exactness):
%%   The falsifiability criterion asked the draft to state "nominal closed-row
%%   equality" as well as closed-to-open widening. The draft now has:
%%     - row INCLUSION as a judgement (§3), constraint-emission-aware
%%       (Reading B), using the compatibility tail judgement ⊆₊ (§4);
%%     - row EQUALITY as a CONSTRAINT FORM (§10), with role-subsumption
%%       on that constraint form (§11);
%%     - a closed-row EQUALITY JUDGEMENT distinct from inclusion (§6),
%%       using the STRICT tail equality judgement ≃₊ (§7), so that nominal
%%       equivalence can be stated precisely without collapsing closedness
%%       or relying on bidirectional inclusion.
%%   The gap flagged by the Reading B decision is now closed: the draft can
%%   state nominal closed-row equality without stepping on itself, because
%%   the equality judgement's exactness condition (≃₊) is distinct from
%%   inclusion's compatibility condition (⊆₊), and the two are kept
%%   consistent by the consistency rule in §7.
%%
%% Remaining open question (not a gap, but a design judgment):
%%   The equality judgement's exactness condition is strict: two open rows
%%   with different tail variables are NOT equal, even if both tails are
%%   "open." This is correct for nominal equality, but it means the equality
%%   judgement cannot fire until the tail variables are unified by the
%%   constraint layer. Whether this is too restrictive for the plugin's use
%%   cases — i.e., whether the plugin ever needs to state equality on two
%%   open rows before their tails are unified — is a question for the plugin
%%   design, not for this draft. The draft commits to the exactness
%%   condition, not to having verified that it is sufficient.
%%
%% Alignment checks:
%%   - Row side: OCaml's merge_row_fields / subtype_row / unify_row
%%     semantics, as recorded in OCAML_ROW_TAIL_SUBTYPE_MECHANISM.md.
%%   - Role side: ICFP 2019 Sub rule and the Nom/Rep lattice from
%%     corespec-roles/spec/ett.ott (JSubRole / SubRole).
%%
%% Falsifiability criterion (from ROW_ROLE_JUDGEMENT_SKETCH.md §5):
%% If this draft cannot state closed-to-open widening and nominal
%% closed-row equality without stepping on itself, it is not ready.
%%
%% Plugin-facing falsifiability test:
%%   1. Can the draft state closed-to-open widening without pretending the
%%      tail judgement solves the tail?
%%   2. Can it state a closed-row equality case without collapsing closedness?
%%   3. Can it state which tail shapes the plugin may widen and which it must
%%      defer/reject?
%% If any of those is unclear, the draft is not yet usable as a plugin
%% boundary contract.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% 13. Boundary contract summary (plain terms)
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% This section restates the §2/§3/§4 decisions together with the §6/§7
%% equality-judgement decision as a single boundary contract between the
%% row-shape layer and the constraint layer, so that a reader who skipped
%% the judgement rules can still see what the draft is committing to.
%%
%% The two-layer model:
%%   - The row-shape / representation layer owns row syntax, closed vs open
%%     distinction, known-field decomposition, the widening rules (§8) that
%%     turn a closed row into an open row, and the equality judgement (§6).
%%     It does NOT solve tail variables.
%%   - The constraint layer owns the TWO tail judgements — the compatibility
%%     judgement ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 (§4, used by inclusion) and the strict equality
%%     judgement ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2 (§7, used by equality) — together with constraint
%%     emission and the discharge of unsolved tail relationships. It does NOT
%%     own the row syntax or the field-index layout.
%%   - The shared row-algebra layer (§5) owns the pure row-algebra primitives
%%     (union, difference, label membership, tail construction, closed/open
%%     discrimination) that both layers call into, so neither layer
%%     independently re-derives the algebra.
%%
%% The two tail judgements (the cost of the §6/§7 decision):
%%   - ⊆₊ (compatibility, §4): used by inclusion. ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 holds when the
%%     tails are compatible under widening. Distinct open tails CAN satisfy
%%     ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 and ρ2 ⊆₊ ρ1 simultaneously (mutual widening compatibility).
%%   - ≃₊ (exactness, §7): used by equality. ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2 holds only when the
%%     tails are exactly the same — the same variable, or unified by the
%%     solver. Distinct open tails do NOT satisfy ≃₊.
%%   - Consistency: if ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2 then ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 and ρ2 ⊆₊ ρ1 (§7).
%%     The converse does NOT hold: mutual ⊆₊ does not imply ≃₊.
%%     This is what makes equality distinct from bidirectional inclusion.
%%
%% The hand-off (inclusion, §3):
%%   When the inclusion judgement §3 fires on two open rows with different
%%   tails, the row-shape layer:
%%     1. decomposes the known fields label-by-label
%%     2. emits ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 into the constraint layer as a constraint
%%     3. does NOT attempt to solve ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 itself
%%   The constraint layer then discharges ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 by:
%%     - unifying the two tail variables if they are the same, or
%%     - widening one tail under the §4 tail contract, or
%%     - rejecting the inclusion if the tail shapes are non-comparable
%%
%% The hand-off (equality, §6):
%%   When the equality judgement §6 fires on two open rows with different
%%   tails, the row-shape layer:
%%     1. decomposes the known fields label-by-label with EXACT equality
%%        (not inclusion) on the known fields
%%     2. emits ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2 into the constraint layer as a constraint
%%     3. does NOT attempt to solve ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2 itself
%%   The constraint layer then discharges ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2 by:
%%     - unifying the two tail variables (if unification succeeds and yields
%%       identical variables, ≃₊ holds; otherwise ≃₊ does not hold)
%%   Unlike inclusion, equality does NOT allow widening as a discharge path:
%%   two distinct open tails are never ≃₊, even if they are mutually
%%   ⊆₊-compatible.
%%
%% What the plugin is responsible for:
%%   - inspecting the row shape and decomposing known fields (for both
%%     inclusion and equality)
%%   - emitting tail judgements as constraints when the known-field
%%     decomposition succeeds but the tails are not identical
%%     (⊆₊ for inclusion, ≃₊ for equality)
%%   - rejecting or deferring when the tail shapes are non-comparable
%%     (both sides closed/fixed and not identical)
%%   - maintaining the consistency invariant: whenever it establishes
%%     ρ1 ≃₊ ρ2 for equality, it must also have established ρ1 ⊆₊ ρ2 and
%%     ρ2 ⊆₊ ρ1 for inclusion
%%
%% What the plugin is NOT responsible for:
%%   - solving unification of row-tail variables (that is the solver's job)
%%   - deciding field indices (that is the representation layer's job)
%%   - re-deriving the row algebra independently of the shared layer
%%   - pretending that widening is unconditional (it is gated by the §4
%%     tail contract)
%%   - treating two open rows with different tail variables as equal (the
%%     equality judgement ≃₊ forbids this, even when ⊆₊ allows mutual
%%     compatibility)
%%
%% HKD-awareness note (cross-reference to the immediate-next-step spec):
%%   The judgement vocabulary here is parametric over the entry kind, so it
%%   already accommodates HKD-style rows without a judgement rewrite. The
%%   remaining work is in the type/vocabulary layer and the plugin's row-walk/
%%   evidence-supply layer, not in §3–§7 as stated. See
%%   plans/2026-10-09-plugin-gap-spec.md §2 for the HKD-aware row-vocabulary
%%   sketch the user asked to target, and docs/notes/PHASE2_SKETCH.md for the
%%   Option D / RowType-flavored vocabulary already assumed there.
%%   Concretely, the judgement's entry-kind metavariable stays at @A@ for now;
%%   when the vocabulary becomes HKD-aware, @A@ is read as @f ty@ in the
%%   appropriate entry-kind, and the same ⊆/≃/⊆₊/≃₊ rules apply without
%%   change. That is the design property the review asked for.
%%
%% HKD-awareness note (cross-reference to the immediate-next-step spec):
%%   The judgement vocabulary here is parametric over the entry kind, so it
%%   already accommodates HKD-style rows without a judgement rewrite. The
%%   remaining work is in the type/vocabulary layer and the plugin's row-walk/
%%   evidence-supply layer, not in §3–§7 as stated. See
%%   plans/2026-10-09-plugin-gap-spec.md §2 for the HKD-aware row-vocabulary
%%   sketch the user asked to target, and docs/notes/PHASE2_SKETCH.md for the
%%   Option D / RowType-flavored vocabulary already assumed there.
%%   Concretely, the judgement's entry-kind metavariable stays at @A@ for now;
%%   when the vocabulary becomes HKD-aware, @A@ is read as @f ty@ in the
%%   appropriate entry-kind, and the same ⊆/≃/⊆₊/≃₊ rules apply without
%%   change. That is the design property the review asked for.
%%
%% Dependency direction:
%%   - row-shape layer → shared row-algebra layer (calls in)
%%   - constraint layer  → shared row-algebra layer (calls in)
%%   - row-shape layer → constraint layer (hands off both ⊆₊ and ≃₊
%%     judgements)
%%   - constraint layer  → row-shape layer (no direct dependency; only
%%     receives emitted constraints)
%%   - shared row-algebra layer → neither consumer (pure, no dependency)
%%
%% The two tail judgements are the explicit cost the session decision
%% accepted for having a distinct equality judgement with tail exactness.
%% The draft commits to stating that cost, not to having minimized it.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% end of draft
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
